+---
+title: "click.Duration param type: parsing rules and doc wiring"
+author: HarzerHeribert
+date: 2026-10-15
+cost: { minutes: 3, tokens: 45208 }
+touches:
+  - docs/api.md
+  - docs/parameter-types.md
+  - src/click/__init__.py
+  - src/click/types.py
+  - tests/test_types.py
+---
+
+## Decided
+- `click.Duration` (src/click/types.py) accepts a `timedelta` unchanged, or a string that is either plain ASCII digits (seconds) or ordered `<int>d`, `<int>h`, `<int>m`, `<int>s` groups, each optional. Everything else fails with "{value!r} is not a valid duration.", and that includes ints such as `default=60`, which the spec requires. A default must be a timedelta or a string.
+- One regex is applied with `fullmatch`. The plain-seconds form is the first alternative and the unit groups are the second. The empty string is rejected separately because the all-optional unit form matches "".
+
+## Tried and ruled out
+- Using `re.IGNORECASE` alone: in Unicode mode it lets "ſ" (long s) match `s`, and `\d` matches non-ASCII digits. The fix is `re.ASCII | re.IGNORECASE`.
+
+## Watch out
+- `timedelta(...)` raises OverflowError for huge values (for example "1000000000d"), and `int()` raises ValueError past 4300 digits. Both are caught and turned into the normal `self.fail` error.
+- A new type needs an `autoclass` entry in docs/api.md as well as in docs/parameter-types.md, because the parameter-types listing uses `:noindex:` and the canonical target lives in api.md.
+- The default pytest run reports about 31k "deselected": pyproject's `addopts = "-m 'not stress'"` skips the stress tests. Nothing is broken.
